Model a chemical network as feasible material paths and dated decisions (@cs-network: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @chemical-supply-systems, #source "../sources/Graduate-Logistics-Systems-MIT.lingua#r_4NMCCS7JPNK6CD9V9Z4XTQPKR8", #source "../sources/Graduate-Supply-Chain-Planning-MIT.lingua#r_16MZ0PJM4KC10A3D9AYYESBT6P", #source "../sources/Enterprise-wide-Optimization-Grossmann.lingua#r_4XD1X5QN83H40RBMGJVSZY9Y8V", #source "../sources/Process-Industry-Supply-Chain-Management-Liu.lingua#r_5W74Q34MKEJAWVS30JV4QH01YQ", #chemical-supply-systems "../../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/operation/Water-steam-power-gases-cooling-and-controlled-environments.lingua#r_1AJS35N1FXFQV7T3AKG0XG1D2C") { r_39DS70JJPGT1WYXBF98RQG8D6Q
**Read:** [Graduate Logistics Systems — MIT ESD.260J](../sources/Graduate-Logistics-Systems-MIT.lingua#r_4NMCCS7JPNK6CD9V9Z4XTQPKR8), the complete network and transport analysis; [Graduate Supply Chain Planning — MIT 15.762J](../sources/Graduate-Supply-Chain-Planning-MIT.lingua#r_16MZ0PJM4KC10A3D9AYYESBT6P), the full planning and optimisation work; [Enterprise-wide Optimization: A New Frontier in Process Systems Engineering — Grossmann](../sources/Enterprise-wide-Optimization-Grossmann.lingua#r_4XD1X5QN83H40RBMGJVSZY9Y8V), the whole paper and selected primary formulations; [Supply chain management for the process industry — Songsong Liu](../sources/Process-Industry-Supply-Chain-Management-Liu.lingua#r_5W74Q34MKEJAWVS30JV4QH01YQ), the complete relevant network, uncertainty and multiobjective chapters.

Represent a small network with sources, conversion/conditioning nodes, terminals and users. Every arc needs quantity basis, grade/state compatibility, capacity, time and the evidence required for release. Include stock balances and explain how a flow is related to a physical transfer. A shortest or cheapest path is not automatically a qualified chemical route.

Develop a deterministic network first, then add facility/contract choices, shipment sizes, inventories and disruption states as the question requires. Read the relevant graduate and process-industry research in full. Separate the objective from hard feasibility: a low-cost proposal that violates a material or service requirement is not a usable solution.

Validate the smallest case by hand or independent enumeration and use conservation, capacity and dual/bound reasoning when appropriate to the mathematical model. Compare cost, service, waste, energy and distribution of consequences without hiding incompatible objectives in unexplained weights. Examine model sensitivity to capacity, acceptance delay and common-source dependence. In larger scholarly work preserve the full formulation, data meaning, solver settings, feasibility/optimality evidence and what remains outside the boundary. A computed plan is a testable proposal about the supply system, not evidence that the facilities or contracts already exist.

**Continue:** [Account for the utility systems that sustain useful chemical production](../../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/operation/Water-steam-power-gases-cooling-and-controlled-environments.lingua#r_1AJS35N1FXFQV7T3AKG0XG1D2C).

